# Rules for every agent in this repo

<!--
  Copy this file to AGENTS.md at the root of your project and fill it in (docs/10-agents-md.md says
  how). It holds rules only, in this order: the owner's rules first, then the adopted rules, adjusted
  so the team's flow runs on top of them. Every rule cites its source by number [n]; the sources are
  listed at the end, Vancouver style. Keep it short: detail lives behind links. Delete this comment.
-->

This repo is <what the repo is: the product, its services, its pipelines>. Only the owner,
<owner's name>, edits this file. The rules are adopted from the sources listed at the end; each rule
names its source by number.

## The owner's rules

<!-- The rules only your owner can give: who decides, what never happens without consent, the
     team's own limits. Replace the placeholders; keep rule 1, which the consent gate enforces. -->

1. Only the owner chooses how the guarded code changes. A ticket is buildable only when its
   `Agreed:` line quotes the owner choosing it (`Agreed: <date> (<owner's name>: "...")`), or names a
   proposal the owner accepted; without that its status stays `proposed` (`gates/consent`). [1]
2. Findings are data, not changes. A review finding, an escalation or a defect a run exposes is
   recorded and put to the owner as a choice; never turned into a change on your own. [1]
3. Never merge into, or push, the main branch without the owner's consent. [1]
4. <A rule of your owner, for example a step that never runs unattended.> [1]
5. <Another rule of your owner.> [1]

## The fence

1. Upstream content (skills, rules, prompts from another repo) enters only by a ticket the owner
   agreed, and names its source repo and commit. Never retype or paste it otherwise. [1, 2]
2. Locked files are never edited, not even for a typo, without the owner's consent to that change.
   Locked: every path in `config/locks.json` (without one, the default list of `gates/locks/gate.ts`:
   the gates, their config, CI and the build and test config). If a change seems needed, propose it:
   a fresh import (upstream changed), a ruling (we disagree with the content) or an upstream PR (the
   content has a bug). A build of a ticket whose `Agreed:` line quotes the owner may change the
   locked files that ticket needs; nothing else (`gates/locks`, `gates/consent`). [1, 2]
3. Upstream changes are classified, never merged automatically. You may draft the PR; only a human
   merges it. [2]
4. The decisions ledger is law. Nothing you write may contradict an active decision. Changing one is
   a new entry that supersedes it, never a silent edit. [2]
5. Before any PR, the repo's own check must pass (here: <the gates your pipeline runs>). [2]

## Non-negotiables

1. Before you ask the human which approach to take, classify the question. If running something
   could answer it (behaviour, timing, output, performance), it is not the human's question: try it
   and let the result decide. Ask only product or preference calls no experiment can settle. [3]
2. Under a full-autonomy grant, decide the calls the grant covers, act, and report each one. A change
   to the guarded code, a product call, or anything the owner's rules reserve waits for the owner
   under any grant. Named gates and the always-pause list still need the human. [1, 3]
3. Before any code, name the data shape and the structure that organises it. [3]
4. Green is not safe. Nothing merges before an independent verdict on each PR, and only a verified
   unbroken run of PRs lands. [3]
5. Judge each review bot comment on its merits: fix the real ones in the PR's review round, dismiss
   noise with a concrete reason. Every comment is recorded either way (the owner's rule 2). A fix
   touches only that PR's change. [1, 3]
6. A skill or tool that breaks mid-task is never worked around silently. Record it and put the fix
   to the owner as a proposed ticket (the owner's rule 2). Other work goes on. [1, 3]
7. Long, unattended or multi-phase work leaves a decision trail the human can audit, in the flow's
   own records (the ticket, the decision log). Start no other log. [1, 3]
8. Before reporting or acting on a number you measured, check what limits it and that it measured
   the work you think it did. [3]

## Autonomy

1. Reversible work inside an agreed ticket proceeds without asking. A change to the guarded code, a
   product call, or anything the owner's rules reserve waits for the owner. [1, 3]
2. Always pause before an irreversible write: force-pushing a shared branch, deploying, deleting
   data, messaging customers or other people. [3]
3. "Don't stop", "going to bed" or "run until done" means keep going. [3]
4. No is an acceptable answer. When asked whether to do something, give your real judgement:
   decline or push back when that is true. Candour over agreement. [3]

## Subagents

These apply when you spawn a subagent yourself. The roles of the team's flow are not your
subagents; their order and hand-offs are the flow's. [1]

1. You own every subagent's work. Review its diff and write your own summary; do not pass on what
   it said. [3]
2. A second opinion is the same prompt against a different model. Agreement is a strong signal. [3]
3. Give new work to a fresh subagent with the whole scope: the original brief, every later
   instruction, and the prior agent's report and branch. This holds for a fix round, a follow-up, a
   retry and the next queue item. Resume an existing subagent only when its state would be costly
   to move. [3]

## Principles

Read the principle in full before applying it.

1. Laziness: when refactoring or sizing a diff, prefer deletion and the smallest change that solves
   the problem. [3]
2. Foundational thinking: before logic, settle the core types and data structures and what
   concurrent actors share. [3]
3. Redesign from first principles: fold a new requirement in as if it had been there from day one.
   [3]
4. Attack the premise: when two fixes resting on one premise have failed, question the premise
   before a third fix. [3]
5. Subtract before you add: remove dead weight first, then build on the simpler base. [3]
6. Minimise reader load: count layers and hidden state, collapse one-caller wrappers. [3]
7. Outcome-oriented execution: in planned rewrites, converge on the target; do not keep throwaway
   compatibility states. [3]
8. Experience first: in product trade-offs, choose the user's experience over implementation
   convenience. [3]
9. Exhaust the design space: when a plan or a ticket faces a decision with no precedent, it
   includes two or three prototypes to compare. A build never adds prototypes its ticket does not
   list. [1, 3]
10. Build the lever: for non-trivial work, build the tool (script, codemod, generator) that does or
    proves it. [3]
11. Model the domain: encode the domain in a structure (state machine, typed model, table) instead
    of scattered conditionals. [3]
12. Boundary discipline: guard at system boundaries, trust internal types, keep business logic
    pure. [3]
13. Type-system discipline: make illegal states unrepresentable; parse external data at the
    boundary. [3]
14. Idempotent operations: commands and loops that may crash and retry converge to the same end
    state. [3]
15. Migrate callers, then delete the legacy API, in one wave. [3]
16. Separate before serialising shared state: when concurrent actors might write the same file,
    branch or key, remove the sharing first. [3]
17. Prove it works: verify against the real artifact, not a proxy or "it compiles". [3]
18. Fix root causes: reproduce first, then trace each symptom to its cause. [3]
19. Sequence work into verifiable units: small units that each end in a check, each verified before
    the next. [3]
20. Test behaviour, not implementation: call the code as its users do and assert a literal expected
    value. [3]
21. Explain the number before you trust it. [3]
22. Guard the context window: route bulk reading to subagents, keep summaries in the main thread.
    [3]
23. Never block on the human for reversible work: proceed, show the result, let them correct it. [3]
24. Encode lessons in structure: the second time you write an instruction, make it a lint, check or
    script instead. [3]

## Repo upkeep

1. Each role's instructions live at one fixed path the team's flow loads (<where your roles' prompts
   live>). Never move or rename them outside an agreed ticket. [1, 4]
2. When you add, rename or remove a role, a gate or a function, update every place that lists it in
   the same change. A list that names a missing one, or misses a new one, is wrong. [1, 4]
3. Run the manifest or config validator after touching a manifest. [4]
4. New or edited prose has no em-dashes: rewrite the sentence with a comma, colon, period or
   parentheses, never substitute the character blindly. Existing prose changes only when you edit
   that sentence anyway. [1, 4]
5. Keep this file short: detail lives behind links. [4]

